  "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=2f(4x+8)=2f(4(x+2))\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=2f(4(x+2)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(4x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215685/assets/80978c68384f9837295ad48a8ea44beff351116c4bb1b4a6a79ecfe6f40136f4.png)\n\n- Then, translate it to the left by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=f(4(x+2)).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215685/assets/eb1cf555fdbd31d88d695a166ef646396bbd38a160f23331c7775bfeb1bc3820.png)\n\n- Finally, stretch it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=2f(4(x+2)).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215685/assets/89cd8b7a309924a5d7a4ca65539059521d07cf619f5751c400622094ba6e69e4.png)",
